.TH "SKARN-SETUP" "1" "2026-06-23" "skarn 0.38.0" "Skarn Manual"
.SH NAME
skarn\-setup \- Wire the guard hook into detected assistants, then self\-test
it
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBskarn\fR \fBsetup\fR [options]
.SH DESCRIPTION
Wire the skarn guard hook into detected AI coding assistants, then verify it
with the guard self\-test. Setup merges its entries into each assistant's
existing config, backs up every file before changing it, and \-\-remove drops
those entries again. A config file that is itself a symlink is written through
at user scope, to the file it names, and the link stays in place; a link whose
file is missing, or whose directory setup cannot write, is refused on install,
and \-\-remove reports a link whose file is missing as unchanged. At \-\-scope
project setup refuses that file, and a file behind a symlinked directory,
instead. A config file setup creates is readable by its owner only on macOS
and Linux, and an existing file keeps its mode.
.PP
Guided onboarding: detect installed AI coding assistants (Claude Code, Cursor,
Codex CLI, Copilot CLI, Gemini CLI, Grok Build, Copilot in Code, Cline, the
Kiro CLI), merge the skarn guard hook into each one's native config in audit
mode, and prove the wiring with the guard self\-test. Copilot in Code gets one
hook file per profile, <profile>\ehooks\eskarn.json, for every profile under
the CopilotCodingHome directories of %LOCALAPPDATA% on Windows and under
~/Library/Application Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/ on
macOS; on either system no matching guard event was observed from the profile
file on Copilot app `158.0.4370.33`, while Copilot app `156.0.4314.58` on
Windows ran it; its folder projects, measured on Windows and on macOS, read
the .github/hooks/skarn.json that \-\-agent copilot \-\-scope project writes,
so \-\-agent copilot\-code refuses \-\-scope project. \-\-scope project writes
committed, shareable configs whose commands are portable by construction: a
PATH\-guarded invocation and a literal $HOME log path, never this machine's
absolute paths. Gemini CLI and Grok Build are the one honest exception,
because each reads a single cross\-platform command field: a file committed
from a POSIX machine carries the POSIX form, and a native\-Windows teammate's
hook then fails open with a warning instead of running. Grok's project hooks
additionally wait on a /hooks\-trust grant. Cline is the other exception, for
a different reason: a project run writes only the pair for the platform it
runs on, the extensionless PreToolUse and UserPromptSubmit on POSIX or the
\&.ps1 files on Windows, so a .clinerules/hooks/ directory committed from one
platform holds no hook a teammate's Cline on the other platform runs, and
doctor on that machine does not count the committed files as protection. On
native Windows, or with a \-\-command that names a drive or UNC path, setup
still writes the Claude Code and Cursor hooks, which have no Windows arm, and
prints one warning line when doctor's Windows reading finds no hook wired.
Cursor runs that hook through PowerShell, which does not parse its POSIX form,
so the hook fails open and actions run unscanned. Claude Code runs it through
Git Bash when that is installed, which skarn has not validated, and otherwise
through PowerShell, where the guard never starts and a tool call can be
blocked instead. \-\-plugin commits the claude plugin requirement instead of
hook entries; the two project routes are mutually exclusive, and each removes
the other's skarn\-owned entries so the guard can never fire twice. Existing
foreign hooks are never touched. Every modified file is backed up first
(*.skarn\-bak.<timestamp>, carrying the target's own permissions) and
rewritten atomically, and a JSON target file that does not parse as JSON is
refused rather than overwritten. At \-\-scope project skarn also refuses a
target that is a symlink, that sits behind a symlinked directory, or whose
path it cannot examine at all, before anything is read or created: a committed
symlink can name any file on the machine, and following it would copy that
file's bytes into a backup beside it and rewrite it in place. At user scope
setup writes through a symlinked config to the file it names and leaves the
link in place, because a dotfiles manager legitimately puts one there; it
refuses a link whose file is missing or whose directory it cannot write,
\-\-remove reports a link whose file is missing as unchanged, and a rewrite
that fails or is rolled back after its verify read leaves no backup behind. A
Copilot in Code hook file reached through a link whose target is a network
path is refused before anything is read or created. When setup cannot open a
Copilot in Code coding home or profile folder, or finds a link where it looks
for profiles, it prints one stderr line per folder, at most eight and then a
count, and goes on with the profiles it found and the other agents; an absent
coding home prints nothing. A JSON config file setup creates is readable by
its owner only on macOS and Linux, while an existing file keeps its mode; on
Windows a new file takes the permissions of the directory it is created in. On
a terminal setup runs an interactive wizard: the mode prompt accepts audit,
enforce, or an empty line for audit, each install prompt accepts y, yes, n,
no, or an empty line for yes, and any other answer is asked again. \-\-yes
accepts everything and \-\-print writes nothing. The result names the mode it
wired, and whether an enforce request runs audit because the machine is
unlicensed or its license is on the free tier. Cline gets executable
PreToolUse and UserPromptSubmit files with mode 0755 on POSIX, or
PreToolUse.ps1 and UserPromptSubmit.ps1 on Windows. At user scope setup writes
only ~/Documents/Cline/Hooks/, which both runtimes read. Documents is the
literal folder joined to HOME, and CLINE_DIR does not move it. Setup never
writes ~/.cline/hooks/, because installing in both directories would make the
CLI run two skarn hooks per call. At project scope it writes
\&.clinerules/hooks/. Setup never creates PostToolUse, but \-\-update and
\-\-remove include a recognized PostToolUse file already there. Those
operations accept only a file setup recognizes as its current render or the
older hand\-copied plugin file; they refuse other guard\-invoking scripts. The
extension reads the files on its next hook event; reload the window if it does
not. Restart a running cline CLI session. The Kiro CLI gets two surfaces,
because its two harnesses read hooks from different places: the hook file
~/.kiro/hooks/skarn.json, which the V3 harness loads in every session, and the
guard merged into every agent config in ~/.kiro/agents/, because the V2
harness runs hooks only from the agent config a session selects. $KIRO_HOME
moves both when it names a directory of its own. Setup reads each agent config
the way kiro\-cli agent upgrade \-\-dry\-run does: it merges into a
V2\-format, out\-of\-sync or upgraded agent in the form that agent's hooks
value takes, skips a V3\-only profile on install while \-\-update and
\-\-remove still maintain a Skarn entry it carries, and refuses a file Kiro
does not read as an agent config. Setup never creates an agent config, so a
machine with none gets the hook file and a skipped row saying that V2 sessions
run unscanned. Kiro reads a single command field on every platform, so a
committed .kiro/hooks/skarn.json or agent config carries the same caveat as
Gemini and Grok. Start a new kiro\-cli session to load the hooks; in a
workspace, an interactive V3 session runs only once the workspace is trusted.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-agent\fR \fIname\fR
Target assistants, repeatable or comma\-separated. Default: all detected (an
assistant is detected when its config directory exists or its CLI is on PATH,
and Copilot in Code when one of its profiles exists); naming an assistant
explicitly also targets it when undetected, except copilot\-code, which setup
refuses when it finds no profile and names where it looked. all targets every
assistant, with copilot\-code only when a profile exists at user scope and
cline only when detected. Cline is detected when ~/.cline (or CLINE_DIR), the
data directory (CLINE_DATA_DIR, else CLINE_DIR/data, else ~/.cline/data), or
~/Documents/Cline is a directory, or cline is on PATH. An explicit \-\-agent
cline writes regardless. Kiro is detected when ~/.kiro/agents (or
$KIRO_HOME/agents) is a directory or kiro\-cli is on PATH; ~/.kiro alone does
not count, because the Kiro IDE may create it. One of: claude, cursor, codex,
copilot, gemini, grok, all, copilot\-code, cline, kiro.
.TP
\fB\-\-mode\fR \fImode\fR
Guard mode written into the hook commands. Audit records would\-be verdicts to
the guard log without blocking; start there, review the log, then flip to
enforce. Enforce needs a paid\-tier license to actually block; an unlicensed
guard, or one on the free tier, runs audit\-only regardless. One of: audit,
enforce. The default is audit.
.TP
\fB\-\-strict\fR
Add \-\-strict to the generated guard commands (gates Read operations on the
hosts that do not gate them by default, and turns the ask the guard gives an
event it cannot fully classify into a deny).
.TP
\fB\-\-log\fR \fIpath\fR
Guard log path wired into the hook commands: a SKARN_GUARD_LOG= prefix on the
POSIX arms and on Copilot's powershell arm, and a \-\-log flag on the Codex
commandWindows arm, which Codex may run under either PowerShell or cmd.exe and
where no shell\-syntax prefix parses under both. Pass none to disable logging.
At \-\-scope project skarn refuses an explicit path, because a committed
config runs on every teammate's machine and carries only the portable
per\-user default. A path with a parent\-directory segment or a control
character is refused at any scope, since the guard refuses it at runtime and
the hook would write nothing. An explicit path reaches only the arms whose
host could have that location: a POSIX\-absolute path reaches the POSIX arms,
a drive\-qualified or UNC path reaches the Windows arm, and the other arm
keeps the portable default rather than a destination its host cannot resolve.
A path that does reach the Codex Windows arm may use only letters, digits and
~ / \e . \- _ : ; anything else is refused rather than escaped, because a bare
argument both shells must parse has no single correct escaping. The default is
~/.cache/skarn/guard\-<agent>\-audit.jsonl.
.TP
\fB\-\-command\fR \fIpath\fR
The guard executable invoked by the hook commands. At user scope the default
resolves this binary's absolute path, preferring the stable Homebrew
<prefix>/bin symlink over the versioned Cellar path, because GUI\-launched
hosts like Cursor do not inherit brew's PATH; pass skarn to opt into
PATH\-relative. On Windows a POSIX hook arm carries that path with forward
slashes (D:/tools/skarn.exe), the spelling Git Bash runs and setup recognizes
on the next run, so a re\-run reports unchanged and \-\-update keeps one entry
per hook; the PowerShell form of a Gemini or Grok hook keeps backslashes. An
entry an earlier setup wrote with backslashes on that arm is replaced whole on
the next run when its Windows arm runs skarn. At project scope the default is
the bare name skarn, and a value containing a path separator is refused: a
committed config must resolve skarn from PATH on every machine. A Cline hook
takes the bare name skarn or an absolute path to an executable named skarn
(skarn.exe on Windows), and on Windows a path that holds [ is refused, because
the Windows PowerShell call operator reads it as wildcard syntax and the hook
never starts skarn. A Windows Cline hook whose command or log path holds a
non\-ASCII character starts with a UTF\-8 byte\-order mark, because Windows
PowerShell 5.1 reads a script without one in the ANSI code page and misreads
the path. The default is this binary's absolute path.
.TP
\fB\-\-scope\fR \fIscope\fR
Write user\-level configs (~/.claude/settings.json, ~/.cursor/hooks.json,
~/.codex/hooks.json, ~/.copilot/hooks/skarn.json, ~/.gemini/settings.json,
~/.grok/hooks/skarn.json, the two Cline hook files in
~/Documents/Cline/Hooks/, ~/.kiro/hooks/skarn.json and the agent configs in
~/.kiro/agents/) or project\-level ones (./.claude, ./.cursor, ./.codex,
\&./.github/hooks/skarn.json, ./.gemini, ./.grok/hooks/skarn.json,
\&./.clinerules/hooks/, ./.kiro/hooks/skarn.json and the agent configs in
\&./.kiro/agents/ relative to the working directory), meant to be committed and
shared. The two scopes write different command shapes on purpose: user scope
bakes this binary's absolute path, immune to a GUI host's missing PATH, and
project scope emits a portable PATH\-guarded command (if command \-v skarn
\&...; then ...; else exit 0; fi) with a literal $HOME log path, so the
committed file runs unmodified on every teammate's machine and exits 0 where
skarn is not installed. Gemini and Grok are the two exceptions to that
promise, because each reads a single command field on every platform: the
committed POSIX form does not run under the PowerShell a native\-Windows
teammate's host uses, the hook exits non\-zero, the host treats that as a
warning and continues, and that machine is unprotected until it wires its own.
The same caveat therefore applies to a committed .grok/hooks/skarn.json, whose
project hooks additionally run only once the folder is trusted with
/hooks\-trust. Cline's project scope writes only the running platform's pair
(the extensionless files on POSIX, the .ps1 files on Windows), so a committed
\&.clinerules/hooks/ directory covers teammates on that platform only. The Kiro
CLI also reads a single command field, so a committed .kiro/hooks/skarn.json
or agent config carries the same Windows caveat. Copilot honors $COPILOT_HOME
as its user\-scope root, grok honors $GROK_HOME, and kiro honors $KIRO_HOME
under Kiro's own fallback rule. One of: user, project. The default is user.
.TP
\fB\-\-plugin\fR
With \-\-scope project, claude only: write the committed plugin requirement
(the skarn entry in extraKnownMarketplaces plus
enabledPlugins["skarn\-guard@skarn"]) instead of hook entries, so every
contributor who opens the repo installs the skarn\-guard plugin from the
public marketplace. Mutually exclusive with the hooks route in both
directions: \-\-plugin removes any skarn\-owned hook entries it previously
wrote, and a later hooks\-route run removes the plugin keys, so the guard can
never fire twice per event. Refused at user scope, for non\-claude assistants,
and combined with \-\-update, \-\-mode, \-\-strict, \-\-log, or \-\-command,
because the plugin ships its own hook files. Foreign marketplaces and plugins
are never touched.
.TP
\fB\-\-print\fR
Print the final per\-assistant config to stdout and write nothing; the
paste\-it\-yourself and fleet\-template path. At a terminal, a printed hook
script has its control bytes shown as ? and is cut at 16 KiB; redirected, it
is printed byte for byte.
.TP
\fB\-\-format\fR \fIfmt\fR
With \-\-print: json emits one {agent, path, content} envelope per assistant
for machine consumption. One of: text, json. The default is text.
.TP
\fB\-y\fR, \fB\-\-yes\fR
Non\-interactive: accept every prompt's default. Required when running without
a terminal.
.TP
\fB\-\-update\fR
Only touch assistants whose config already carries skarn entries; the
audit\-to\-enforce flip is skarn setup \-\-update \-\-mode enforce. An owned
entry is replaced whole: any field you added to it is dropped, and a field a
newer template stopped writing stays gone. The one exception is a hook\-call
timeout you tuned yourself (timeout, or timeoutSec on copilot), carried across
only when its entry pairs unambiguously with the replacement and the value is
a positive integer within the host's range, and otherwise reset to the
template default. A hook you added inside skarn's own matcher group is kept
where you put it: skarn replaces its own entry beside yours instead of adding
a second group to the event.
.TP
\fB\-\-remove\fR
Surgically remove skarn\-owned hook entries, dropping any matcher groups and
event arrays they leave empty. Foreign hooks are untouched, the file itself is
never deleted, and backups from earlier runs are kept.
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Print a machine\-readable result summary (per assistant: path, action, backup,
self\-test result) instead of the text report.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fB./.claude/settings.json, ./.cursor/hooks.json, ./.codex/hooks.json, ./.github/hooks/skarn.json, ./.gemini/settings.json\fR
A repo's committed project\-scope hook configs (claude, cursor, codex,
copilot, gemini), written by skarn setup \-\-scope project and read by doctor
as each host's project precedence layer whenever it runs in that directory.
Copilot in Code reads the copilot file in a folder project; in a working
directory holding a .git entry doctor reports it unverified for copilot\-code,
because a repository project was not measured. Portable by construction: the
committed command resolves skarn from PATH at run time and exits 0 where skarn
is not installed. Gemini reads one command field on every platform, so its
committed POSIX command fails open on a native\-Windows teammate; Gemini also
runs hooks from a project file only once that folder is trusted.
.TP
\fB~/.gemini/settings.json\fR
Gemini CLI's user settings: MCP server definitions read by vet, and the hooks
object skarn setup \-\-agent gemini merges the guard into and doctor reads as
the user precedence layer. When GEMINI_CLI_HOME is nonempty, vet, assess,
inventory, and the MCP vet_configs tool read
$GEMINI_CLI_HOME/.gemini/settings.json instead. Setup and doctor still use
~/.gemini/settings.json.
.TP
\fB~/.grok/hooks/skarn.json\fR
Grok Build's user hook file for skarn, written whole by skarn setup \-\-agent
grok and read by doctor as the setup\-owned half of grok's user layer. Grok
loads every *.json in ~/.grok/hooks, so skarn owns a file of its own there and
never merges into a foreign one; doctor folds the directory's other files into
the same layer, so a hook wired by hand under another name still counts.
$GROK_HOME moves the whole directory.
.TP
\fB.grok/hooks/skarn.json\fR
Grok Build's project hook file for skarn, written by skarn setup \-\-agent
grok \-\-scope project and meant to be committed. Grok runs a project hook
only after the folder is trusted (/hooks\-trust, or launching with \-\-trust);
until then it skips the file silently, so doctor reports that layer as inert
rather than as protection.
.TP
\fB%LOCALAPPDATA%\eMicrosoft\eCopilot\eUser Data\eCopilotCodingHome\e<profile>\ehooks\eskarn.json, the same file under the MSIX twin of that directory (Windows)\fR
The skarn\-owned hook file of one Copilot in Code profile. skarn setup
\-\-agent copilot\-code writes it for every profile it finds, a directory
inside a CopilotCodingHome directory that holds session\-state, hooks or
data.db, plus a $COPILOT_HOME that names one, with the usual backup, atomic
write and verify. Discovery does not count or open a marker or a profile
folder that is a link, a junction included, and data.db counts only as a file;
a $COPILOT_HOME that names a Code profile can still name a linked folder
unless the link points at a network path. Setup prints a line and doctor's
agent_config_copilot_code names each such link, each coding home or profile
folder skarn cannot open, and a coding home or named profile reached through a
link to a network path; the check then warns that whether a profile there
carries a guard hook is unknown, and appears even when no profile was found. A
profile's hooks folder may be a link to a local folder, and setup writes
through it and doctor reads through it; when the hooks folder, or a folder on
the way to the hook file, is a link whose target is a network path, setup
refuses that profile before anything is read or created (refused
network\-target in the JSON result) and doctor reads nothing through it and
warns that the hook file is reached through a link to a network path. The
profile's settings.json is read through a link to a local file or folder; when
it, or a folder on the way to it, is a link whose target is a network path,
doctor reads nothing through it, reports whether its disableAllHooks silences
the hooks as unknown, and names a command that inspects the link itself. Its
content is the shipped copilot hook file with the guard log moved to
guard\-copilot\-code\-audit.jsonl; the command still pins \-\-agent copilot.
Doctor reads each profile's settings.json for disableAllHooks. Copilot app
`156.0.4314.58` on Windows ran this file; on Windows and macOS no matching
guard event was observed from it on Copilot app `158.0.4370.33`, while the
\&.github/hooks/skarn.json of a folder project ran the hook, so doctor reports a
wired profile file as unverified on every system, never as protection, and
names skarn setup \-\-agent copilot \-\-scope project. On macOS setup and
doctor also find every profile under ~/Library/Application
Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/. On Linux setup and doctor
reach a profile only through a $COPILOT_HOME that names it. Verified against
Copilot app `156.0.4314.58`.
.TP
\fB~/Documents/Cline/Hooks/\fR
Cline's shared user hook directory, read by the extension and the CLI.
Documents is the literal folder joined to HOME; CLINE_DIR does not move it.
Setup writes only this user directory to avoid duplicate CLI invocations,
using executable PreToolUse and UserPromptSubmit files on POSIX or
PreToolUse.ps1 and UserPromptSubmit.ps1 on Windows. Doctor reports each file
as part of the four peer directories.
.TP
\fB~/.cline/hooks/\fR
Cline's CLI\-only user hook directory, relocated to <CLINE_DIR>/hooks when
CLINE_DIR is set. Doctor reads it as a peer of the shared user and two
workspace directories. Setup never writes here, because the CLI also reads
~/Documents/Cline/Hooks/ and would run duplicate hooks.
.TP
\fB.clinerules/hooks/\fR
Cline's shared workspace hook directory, read by the extension and the CLI.
Setup writes PreToolUse and UserPromptSubmit here with \-\-scope project, or
PreToolUse.ps1 and UserPromptSubmit.ps1 on Windows. Doctor reads it as a peer
of both user directories and .cline/hooks/.
.TP
\fB.cline/hooks/\fR
Cline's CLI\-only workspace hook directory. Doctor reads each file as a peer
of the other three hook directories; any cancel wins. Setup writes project
hooks only to .clinerules/hooks/.
.TP
\fB~/.kiro/hooks/skarn.json\fR
The Kiro CLI's user hook file for skarn, a v1 document whose hooks array runs
skarn guard \-\-agent kiro on PreToolUse and UserPromptSubmit. skarn setup
\-\-agent kiro writes it whole, and doctor reads it as the setup\-owned half
of Kiro's user layer. The V3 harness loads every *.json in ~/.kiro/hooks in
every session, so doctor folds the directory's other files into the same
layer; the V2 harness never loads a hook file. An entry with enabled set to
false is skipped by Kiro, so doctor does not count it. $KIRO_HOME moves the
directory.
.TP
\fB.kiro/hooks/skarn.json\fR
The Kiro CLI's workspace hook file for skarn, written by skarn setup \-\-agent
kiro \-\-scope project with a PATH\-guarded command and meant to be committed.
The V3 harness loads it in every session in the workspace; an interactive
session runs only once the workspace is trusted, and headless runs are not
gated.
.TP
\fB~/.kiro/agents/\fR
The Kiro CLI's user agent configs. skarn setup \-\-agent kiro merges the guard
into each one Kiro reads as an agent config: the camelCase hooks object of a
V2\-format or out\-of\-sync agent, or the camelCase hooks array of an agent
Kiro upgraded. It skips a V3\-only profile on install and refuses a file that
is not an agent config. Both harnesses run an agent's hooks only while a
session selects that agent, so doctor names the agent files that carry a skarn
hook and states V2 coverage as those agents only. Kiro skips an entry with
enabled set to false in an upgraded agent's hooks array but runs it in the
object form, and doctor counts each the way Kiro runs it. $KIRO_HOME moves the
directory, and doctor then reports it as unverified for the V2 harness.
.TP
\fB.kiro/agents/\fR
The Kiro CLI's workspace agent configs, which skarn setup \-\-agent kiro
\-\-scope project merges into with a PATH\-guarded command and doctor reads as
a project layer, under the same rules as ~/.kiro/agents/.
.TP
\fB~/Library/Application Support/Microsoft/CopilotAppHost/CopilotCodingHome/<profile>/hooks/skarn.json (macOS)\fR
The skarn\-owned hook file of one Copilot in Code profile on macOS. skarn
setup \-\-agent copilot\-code writes it for every profile under this
CopilotCodingHome directory, and doctor reads it, by the rules the Windows
entry for this file states. On the verified install no profile held a hooks
folder. With the file written there, no matching guard event was observed from
it, while the .github/hooks/skarn.json of a folder project ran the hook, so
doctor on macOS reports a wired profile file as unverified, never as
protection, and names skarn setup \-\-agent copilot \-\-scope project.
Verified against Copilot app `158.0.4370.33`.
